{"id": 4415790, "name": "SALLEY v. MANCHESTER AND AUGUSTA R. R. CO.; ZEIGLER v. SAME", "name_abbreviation": "Salley v. Manchester & Augusta R. R.", "decision_date": "1901-11-29", "docket_number": "", "first_page": "127", "last_page": "130", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "62 S.C. 127"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "55 S. C., 325", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "54 S. C., 484", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "25 S. C., 24", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [8715047], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/25/0024-01"]}, {"cite": "24 S. C., 499", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4384801], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/24/0499-01"]}, {"cite": "36 S. C., 551", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [8716571], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/36/0551-01"]}, {"cite": "40 S. C., 533", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [366373], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/40/0533-01"]}, {"cite": "41 S. C., 50", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4400992], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/41/0050-01"]}, {"cite": "40 P. R., 282", "category": "reporters:state_regional", "reporter": "P. R.,", "case_ids": [8777334], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/pr/40/0282-01"]}, {"cite": "55 S. C., 334", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4409944], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/55/0334-01"]}, {"cite": "54 S. C., 481", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4409048], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/54/0481-01"]}, {"cite": "53 S. C., 448", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [8716567], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/53/0448-01"]}, {"cite": "27 S. C., 64", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "19 S. C., 354", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "5 Rich., 358", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Rich.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "5 S. C., 358", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4369532], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/5/0358-01"]}, {"cite": "54 S. C., 481", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4409048], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/54/0481-01"]}, {"cite": "53 S. C., 448", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [8716567], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/53/0448-01"]}, {"cite": "27 S. C., 64", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": 0}, {"cite": "19 S. C., 354", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": 0}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 317, "char_count": 4864, "ocr_confidence": 0.705, "pagerank": {"raw": 8.713938505143155e-08, "percentile": 0.493527421868997}, "sha256": "8bf2f79fbf1722f4e1baaf1c0d27bbd13d94b17a2c6ddca16344055ca9641320", "simhash": "1:5efdadc5969405d4", "word_count": 874}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T17:15:38.288070+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["SALLEY v. MANCHESTER AND AUGUSTA R. R. CO. ZEIGLER v. SAME."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion was filed July 22, 1901, but held up by order staying remittitur until\nThe opinion of the Court was delivered by\nMr. Justice Pope.\nThe two foregoing actions were heard together in the Circuit Court and also in this Court. These cases were once before in this Court on demurrer to the complaints for failure to state facts sufficient to constitute a cause of action. By the judgment of this Court, such demurrers were overniled; 54 S. C., 481. The actions came on for trial before his Honor, Judge Buchanan, and a jury, verdicts were for plaintiffs. Motions were made for non-suit on the conclusion of plaintiffs\u2019 testimony, and for new trials after verdict rendered.' Both motions were overruled.\nThe defendant now appeals and as its ground for appeal suggests that the Circuit Judge erred in permitting plaintiffs\u2019 witness, Mr. D. J. Salley, to testify as to what the defendant\u2019s engineer, Mr. George Wilson, had testified at the former trials as to his not having, on the evening defendant\u2019s train killed the dogs, rung his bell, sounded his whistle or turned off steam, Mr. Wilson being present in Court. And, also, that the Circuit Judge erred in refusing to grant a non-suit.\nAs to the refusal to grant the nonsuif, an examination of the record convinces us .that there was some testimony as to negligence by the defendant in the evidence offered by the plaintiffs, apart from and independent of that portion of the testimony of Mr. D. J. Salley which purported to state what the engineer, Mr. Wilson, had admitted at the former trial. So the refusal of the non-suit must be sustained.\nLet us now examine the ground of appeal, which assigns error in the ruling by the Circuit Judge whereby the witness, Salley, was allowed to state the admission in his testimony at the former hearing, by the engineer of defendant\u2019s train, which killed the dogs of the two plaintiffs, that no bell was rung, no whistle was sounded, and no steam let off to warn or frighten the three dogs from defendant\u2019s track, at, on or near the trestle. We think this was error. The testimony of the witness was given some time after the killing of the dogs. It was not given as a part of the res gestae. It did not even purport to give all that witness testified at the former trial. Such testimony should not have been allowed, it being objected to by defendant before it was received. Aiken v. Telegraph Co., 5 S. C., 358; Piedmont Manftg. Co. v. C. & G. R. R. Co., 19 S. C., 354; Petrie v. C. & G. R. R. Co., 27 S. C., 64; Garrick v. F. C. & P. R. R. Co., 53 S. C., 448. It follows, therefore, that there was reversible error here, so far as the refusal to grant a new trial was concerned.\nIt is the judgment of this Court, that the judgment of the Circuit Court in both cases be reversed, and that the actions be remitted to the Court below for a new trial.\nThe petition for a rehearing was refused, November 29, 1901, by the following order:\nPER Curiam.\nAfter careful consideration of this petition, we have been unable to find that any material fact or principle of law has either been overlooked or disregarded, and hence there is no ground for a rehearing.\nIt is, therefore, ordered, that this petition be dismissed, and that the stay of the remittitur heretofore granted be revoked.", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Justice Pope. PER Curiam."}], "attorneys": ["Messrs. Moss & Lide and I. T. Barron, for appellant,", "Messrs. I. W. Bowman and H. H. Brunson, contra,"], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "SALLEY v. MANCHESTER AND AUGUSTA R. R. CO. ZEIGLER v. SAME.\n1. Nonsuit. \u2014 There being some evidence of negligence here, nonsuit was properly refused.\n2. Evidence \u2014 Negligence\u2014Master and Servant. \u2014 In an action for damages caused by defendant\u2019s alleged negligence, it is error to permit a witness for plaintiff to detail at a second trial a portion of the testimony of a witness of defendant, its agent and servant, given at the first trial, as to admission of alleged negligent acts by him.\n3. Rehearing refused.\nBefore Buchanan, J., Orangeburg, September, 1900.\nReversed.\nTwo actions: (i) David J. Salley and (2) J. A. Zeigler against Manchester and Augusta Railroad Company, for damages for killing dogs. Prom verdict for plaintiffs, defendant appeals.\nMessrs. Moss & Lide and I. T. Barron, for appellant,\ncite: It is error to admit in evidence only a part of the testimony of a witness at a former trial, the witness being in the Court room: 5 Rich., 358; 19 S. C., 354; 27 S. C., 64; 53 S. C., 448. There is no presumption of negligence from killing a dog by railway train: 54 S. C., 481; 55 S. C., 334; 10 Rich. L,., 58; 40 P. R., 282.\nMessrs. I. W. Bowman and H. H. Brunson, contra,\ncite: Objection below was that the evidence was incompetent because secondary evidence of a record, and not that it was not a part of the res gestae, and the latter objection cannot be considered here: 41 S. C., 50; 40 S. C., 533; 36 S. C., 551; 24 S. C., 499. There being some evidence of negligence, nonsuit was properly refused: 25 S. C., 24; 54 S. C., 484; 55 S. C., 325.\nNovember 29, 1901."}, "file_name": "0127-01", "first_page_order": 135, "last_page_order": 138}